Design a professional, high-fidelity, interactive web application prototype for:

“AI Career Readiness Assistant for International Students in Australia”

PROJECT CONTEXT

This is a Startup Minimum Viable Product (MVP) designed specifically to support international students in Australia with career readiness.

The platform addresses challenges international students may experience when preparing for graduate employment in Australia, including unfamiliarity with Australian resume expectations, interview practices, employer expectations, and limited access to personalised career support.

The MVP must remain focused on TWO core functional capabilities:

1. AI-Powered Resume Feedback
2. AI-Assisted Interview Preparation

Responsible AI must be embedded throughout the experience as a governance and trust layer rather than presented as a separate career-coaching feature.

Do NOT add unrelated features such as:
- job search
- job matching
- job application tracking
- visa or migration advice
- salary prediction
- networking/social media
- courses
- recruitment marketplace
- general-purpose AI chatbot
- employer portal

The purpose is career readiness, not recruitment or employment placement.


DESIGN DIRECTION

Create a minimalist, modern, trustworthy and professional interface.

The visual style should feel appropriate for:
- university students
- international students
- graduate job seekers
- Australian higher education
- professional career development

Avoid making the application look like a generic AI chatbot or overly futuristic AI product.

Use:
- generous white space
- clean typography
- subtle borders
- soft shadows where appropriate
- restrained use of colour
- simple cards
- clear visual hierarchy
- rounded corners used moderately
- accessible contrast
- consistent spacing
- simple professional icons
- minimal visual clutter

Use a neutral/light background with one restrained professional accent colour.

The overall impression should be:
Professional + Supportive + Trustworthy + Simple + Student-friendly.

Design primarily for desktop web at approximately 1440px width, while using responsive components suitable for later adaptation to tablet/mobile.

Follow WCAG 2.2 AA principles where practical.


INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE

Use a simple application navigation structure:

Logo / Product Name

Dashboard
Resume Feedback
Interview Preparation
My Progress
Responsible AI & Privacy

User/Profile menu

Keep navigation minimal.


SCREEN 1 — LANDING / WELCOME PAGE

Create a clean landing page introducing:

“AI Career Readiness Assistant”

Supporting text:
“Personalised AI-powered resume feedback and interview preparation for international students in Australia.”

Primary CTA:
“Get Started”

Secondary CTA:
“Learn How It Works”

Include two feature cards:

Resume Feedback
“Receive personalised feedback to improve your resume for the Australian employment context.”

Interview Preparation
“Practise interview questions and receive constructive AI-assisted feedback.”

Include a small Responsible AI trust section highlighting:

Fairness
Transparency
Privacy
Human Oversight

Include a clear disclaimer that the platform provides career preparation support and does not guarantee employment outcomes.


SCREEN 2 — ONBOARDING

Create a short, low-friction onboarding process.

Ask only for information necessary to personalise career-readiness support.

Fields:

- Preferred name
- Field of study
- Current qualification
- Target job/role
- Industry of interest
- Career stage

Career stage options could include:
- Preparing for graduate employment
- Currently applying
- Preparing for interviews

Do not ask for unnecessary sensitive personal information.

Include:

“Why we ask this”

with a short explanation that the information is used to contextualise feedback.

Include:
Continue
Skip optional information


SCREEN 3 — DASHBOARD

Create a simple personalised dashboard.

Greeting:
“Welcome, [Name]”

Supporting message:
“Continue building your career readiness for the Australian job market.”

Primary cards:

1. Resume Feedback
Upload and review a resume.

CTA:
“Review My Resume”

2. Interview Preparation
Practise interview questions and receive feedback.

CTA:
“Start Interview Practice”

Include a small “Your Progress” section showing:

Resume Review
Interview Practice

Use simple progress indicators rather than gamification.

Include a small Responsible AI notice:
“AI-generated feedback should be reviewed critically and used alongside professional career advice.”


SCREEN 4 — RESUME UPLOAD

Heading:
“Resume Feedback”

Supporting text:
“Upload your resume to receive personalised feedback for the Australian employment context.”

Create a drag-and-drop upload component.

Supported formats:
PDF
DOCX

Optional field:
“Target role or job title”

Optional field:
“Paste a job description”

Primary CTA:
“Analyse Resume”

Include privacy information near the upload component:

“Your resume may contain personal information. Only upload information you are comfortable using for this assessment.”

Provide a visible link:
“Learn how your data is handled”

Include a loading state after analysis begins:
“Analysing your resume…”


SCREEN 5 — AI RESUME FEEDBACK RESULTS

This should be one of the strongest screens in the prototype.

Heading:
“Your Resume Feedback”

At the top, show a concise overview rather than an arbitrary employability score.

Example:
“Your resume communicates relevant experience clearly, but several areas could be strengthened for Australian graduate applications.”

Organise feedback into clear sections/cards:

1. Structure & Formatting
2. Professional Summary
3. Skills & Experience
4. Clarity & Language
5. Relevance to Target Role
6. Suggested Improvements

For individual recommendations, use:

Observation
Why it matters
Suggested improvement

Example:

Observation:
“Your experience descriptions focus mainly on responsibilities.”

Why it matters:
“Employers often benefit from seeing evidence of outcomes and achievements.”

Suggested improvement:
“Where accurate, describe measurable outcomes or specific contributions.”

Do NOT invent employment facts or rewrite experience as if unverified achievements occurred.

Include:
“Copy Suggestion”
“Review Another Section”
“Upload Updated Resume”

Add an AI disclaimer:
“AI-generated feedback may contain inaccuracies. Review recommendations before making changes to your resume.”


SCREEN 6 — INTERVIEW PREPARATION SETUP

Heading:
“Interview Preparation”

Supporting text:
“Practise interview questions relevant to your target role and receive constructive feedback.”

Fields:

Target role
Industry
Interview type

Interview type options:
- Behavioural
- General graduate interview
- Role-specific
- Mixed practice

Difficulty:
- Beginner
- Intermediate
- Advanced

Number of questions:
3 / 5 / 10

Primary CTA:
“Start Practice”

Include an option:
“Use my career profile”

Do not include video analysis or facial/emotion recognition.


SCREEN 7 — INTERVIEW PRACTICE

Create a focused interview simulation.

Display:

“Question 1 of 5”

Example question:
“Tell me about a time you worked effectively as part of a team.”

Provide a large response text area.

Placeholder:
“Type your response here…”

Include optional guidance:
“Consider using the STAR structure: Situation, Task, Action, Result.”

Buttons:
“Submit Response”
“Skip”
“End Practice”

Show progress clearly but keep the interface calm and distraction-free.


SCREEN 8 — INTERVIEW FEEDBACK

After submitting a response, show constructive feedback.

Sections:

What worked well
What could be improved
Response structure
Clarity and relevance
Suggested approach

Example feedback structure:

Strength:
“You clearly explained your role within the team.”

Improvement:
“The outcome of your actions could be explained more clearly.”

Suggestion:
“Add one or two sentences explaining the result and what you learned.”

CTA:
“Next Question”

Secondary:
“Try Again”

Avoid ranking personality, accent, ethnicity, emotion or employability.

Feedback should focus on the CONTENT and STRUCTURE of the response.


SCREEN 9 — INTERVIEW SESSION SUMMARY

At the end of the session display:

“Practice Complete”

Include:

Questions completed
Key strengths
Areas for improvement
Suggested next steps

Show progress using simple indicators.

CTA:
“Practice Again”

Secondary CTA:
“Return to Dashboard”

Do not claim that the user is “job ready” or predict interview success.


SCREEN 10 — MY PROGRESS

Create a simple history/progress page.

Sections:

Resume Feedback History
Interview Practice History

Example information:

Resume Review
Date
Target role
Review status

Interview Practice
Date
Role
Questions completed
Areas for improvement

Keep this lightweight because the product is an MVP.


SCREEN 11 — RESPONSIBLE AI & PRIVACY

Create a dedicated transparency page.

Heading:
“Responsible AI & Privacy”

Explain the platform's approach using four cards:

Fairness
“Feedback is designed to focus on career preparation rather than personal characteristics.”

Transparency
“AI-generated recommendations are clearly identified and should be critically reviewed.”

Privacy
“The MVP minimises unnecessary collection of personal information.”

Human Oversight
“AI feedback supports career preparation and does not replace professional career advisers.”

Include:

“How AI is used”

Explain that Generative AI assists with:
- analysing resume content
- generating interview questions
- generating constructive feedback

Include:

“Limitations”

State clearly:
- AI can produce inaccurate or inappropriate recommendations.
- Users should verify important career information.
- The platform does not guarantee employment outcomes.
- Users should seek professional career advice when appropriate.

Reference the design principles conceptually around:
- NIST AI Risk Management Framework
- Australian Privacy Principles
- Australian AI Ethics Principles


USER EXPERIENCE FLOW

Prototype these primary flows:

FLOW A — Resume Feedback

Landing Page
→ Get Started
→ Onboarding
→ Dashboard
→ Resume Feedback
→ Upload Resume
→ AI Analysis
→ Feedback Results
→ Dashboard


FLOW B — Interview Preparation

Dashboard
→ Interview Preparation
→ Configure Practice
→ Interview Question
→ Enter Response
→ AI Feedback
→ Next Question
→ Session Summary
→ Dashboard


FLOW C — Responsible AI

Dashboard
→ Responsible AI & Privacy
→ AI Usage / Limitations / Privacy Information
→ Dashboard


COMPONENT SYSTEM

Create reusable components for:

- Navigation sidebar/header
- Primary button
- Secondary button
- Feature card
- Feedback card
- Progress indicator
- Form fields
- Upload component
- AI disclaimer
- Privacy notice
- Modal/dialog
- Loading state
- Empty state
- Success state
- Error state

Create consistent button, input, card and typography styles.


PROTOTYPE INTERACTIONS

Make the prototype interactive.

Include realistic navigation between screens.

Prototype:

Get Started
Resume upload
Analyse Resume
View feedback
Interview setup
Start Practice
Submit Response
Next Question
Complete Session
Return to Dashboard
Responsible AI information

Use realistic example content rather than Lorem Ipsum.


ERROR AND SAFETY STATES

Design at least these states:

Resume upload failed
Unsupported file type
AI service temporarily unavailable
AI response could not be generated
Empty interview response

Use calm, helpful messages.

Example:

“We couldn’t generate feedback right now. Please try again.”

Avoid technical error codes in the user interface.


PRIVACY-BY-DESIGN REQUIREMENTS

Because users may upload resumes containing personal information:

- clearly communicate when information is being processed
- avoid unnecessary personal-data collection
- provide privacy notices at the point of upload
- do not request visa status, ethnicity, religion, health information, gender or other sensitive attributes unless strictly necessary — they are NOT required for this MVP
- do not display uploaded personal information unnecessarily
- make AI-generated content visibly identifiable


CONTENT STYLE

Use Australian English throughout.

Use:
“personalised”
“organisation”
“analyse”
“labour market”

Tone:
supportive, professional, concise and non-judgemental.

Avoid:
“You are a weak candidate.”
“You will get this job.”
“You are 85% employable.”
“You failed the interview.”

Prefer:
“This response could be strengthened by…”
“Consider providing a more specific example…”
“This section may benefit from…”
“Review this recommendation before applying it.”


IMPORTANT MVP SCOPE CONSTRAINT

This prototype represents a university capstone Startup MVP, not a production commercial platform.

Prioritise demonstrating:

1. Resume feedback
2. Interview preparation
3. Responsible AI
4. Privacy
5. Usability
6. Clear user flow

Do not unnecessarily increase product scope.

The final Figma output should look sufficiently professional for:
- usability testing with international student participants
- demonstration to an academic supervisor
- inclusion in a professional GitHub portfolio
- implementation as a functional Python-based MVP during the next development stage.


FINAL FIGMA DELIVERABLE

Generate:

1. A small design system/style guide
2. Desktop landing page
3. Onboarding
4. Dashboard
5. Resume upload
6. Resume feedback results
7. Interview setup
8. Interview practice
9. Interview feedback
10. Interview session summary
11. Progress page
12. Responsible AI & Privacy page
13. Essential loading/error states
14. Connected interactive prototype flows

Keep the interface minimalist and cohesive. Prioritise usability and clarity over decorative visual elements.